#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179478 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f429709-cb4b-5319-8d2b-0858bc409e89" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curl of a Vector Field Using Linearity Properties"}] :question/id #uuid "3a28f359-71aa-5b9b-b55c-802374e6821f" :question/math-academy-id "e-8745" :question/problem "Calculate $\\nabla\\times(3\\mathbf{F} + \\mathbf{G})$ at the point $(-1,1,0)$ for the vector fields $\\mathbf F$ and $\\mathbf G$, where\n\n$\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z) = 3x\\,\\mathbf{i} + y^2z\\,\\mathbf{j} + 2\\,\\mathbf{k}, \\quad (\\nabla\\times\\mathbf{G})(x,y,z) = 3y^2\\,\\mathbf{i} + 4z\\,\\mathbf{j} + \\mathbf{k}$." :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf F$ and $\\mathbf G$ are vector fields and $k$ is a constant, then we have the following linearity properties for curl:\n\n- $\\nabla \\times (k \\mathbf F) = k \\nabla \\times \\mathbf F$\n- $\\nabla \\times (\\mathbf F + \\mathbf G) = \\nabla \\times \\mathbf F + \\nabla \\times \\mathbf G$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇ \\cdot (3F + G) &= ∇ \\cdot (3F) + ∇ \\cdot G \\\\\n&= 3∇ \\cdot F + ∇ \\cdot G\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to calculate $\\nabla \\times \\mathbf{F}$. In doing so, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇ \\cdot F &= \\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ 3x & y^{2}z & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= [\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2) - \\frac{∂}{∂z}(y^{2}z)]i - [\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2) - \\frac{∂}{∂z}(3x)]j + [\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y^{2}z) - \\frac{∂}{∂y}(3x)]k \\\\\n&= (0 - y^{2})i - (0 - 0)j + (0 - 0)k \\\\\n&=-y^{2}i\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the linearity properties, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇ \\cdot (3F + G) &= 3∇ \\cdot F + ∇ \\cdot G \\\\\n&= 3[- y^{2}i] + [3y^{2}i + 4zj + k] \\\\\n&=-3y^{2}i + 3y^{2}i + 4zj + k \\\\\n&= (3y^{2} - 3y^{2})i + 4zj + k \\\\\n&= 4zj + k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating at the point $(-1,1,0)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(∇ \\cdot (F - G))(-1, 1, 0) &= 4(0)j + k = k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179480 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ffe539af-1253-5ce3-a65b-eb50ed639c52" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curl of a Vector Field Using the Product Rule"}] :question/id #uuid "e27e6735-4d50-5c26-a20d-44454ddcdef3" :question/math-academy-id "e-8931" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y,z) = \\langle 0, y-z, x-y\\rangle$ and the scalar field $f(x,y,z) = x+y$. Given that\n$\\nabla\\times \\mathbf F = \\langle 0,\\,-1,\\,0\\rangle, \\quad \\nabla f = \\langle 1,\\, 1,\\, 0\\rangle$,\ncalculate $\\nabla\\times(f\\,\\mathbf F)$ at the point $(-1,1,3)$." :question/worked-solution "We shall use the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\nabla\\times(f\\,\\mathbf F) = f \\nabla \\times \\mathbf {F}+ \\nabla f \\times \\mathbf F\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute $\\nabla f \\times \\mathbf F$. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇f \\cdot F &= \\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & y - z & x - y\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= [1 \\cdot (x - y) - 0 \\cdot (y - z)]i - [1 \\cdot (x - y) - 0 \\cdot (0)]j + [1 \\cdot (y - z) - 1 \\cdot (0)]k \\\\\n&= (x - y)i + (y - x)j + (y - z)k \\\\\n&= ⟨x - y, y - x, y - z⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇ \\cdot (fF) &= f∇ \\cdot F + ∇f \\cdot F \\\\\n&= (x + y)⟨0,-1, 0⟩ + ⟨x - y, y - x, y - z⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨0, - (x + y), 0⟩ + ⟨x - y, y - x, y - z⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨x - y,-2x, y - z⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇ \\cdot (fF)(-1, 1, 3) &= ⟨(-1) - 1,-2(-1), 1 - 3⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨ - 2, 2,-2⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
